Critical Instruction

Do not assume that asynchronous means faster.

Measure where the current system spends time and determine whether the limiting resource is:

CPU
DB
Kafka
Network
Serialization
Thread Pool
GC
IO
Lock contention

The goal is not maximum concurrency.

The goal is:

Maximum sustainable throughput with predictable latency, bounded resource consumption, durable auditability, reliable Kafka delivery, and no single bottleneck capable of destabilizing the payment-processing platform.

Do not fabricate benchmark results.

If profiling/load-test data is unavailable, clearly distinguish:

observed bottlenecks vs code-level risks vs hypotheses requiring measurement.

For your architecture, I would pay particular attention to audit amplification. If one payment generates 10–20 audit events, the audit subsystem can easily become the real throughput limiter even when the business processing and Kafka consumers are highly concurrent. The strongest design is usually business path → durable DB/outbox → independently batched audit/Kafka workers, with the exact coupling determined by your audit and delivery requirements.
